<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="assignments">
  <title>Assignments</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            There will be <alert>10 written assignments</alert>, and each
            assignment is worth <alert>1 point</alert>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>Assignments are due on <alert>Fridays at 11:59 PM</alert>.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <em>Exception:</em> in the weeks in which we have a midterm exam
            (weeks 3, 6, and 9), the assignment is due on
            <alert>Wednesday at 11:59 PM</alert> instead, since the exams are on
            the Fridays of those weeks. <xref ref="table-assignment-days"/> lists
            the due day for every assignment.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <alert>No late submissions will be accepted</alert> under any
            circumstances, so please plan ahead and do not wait until the last
            minute to upload your work.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <em>Where to find the problems:</em> each assignment is its own
            worksheet in the
            <url href="https://mahmadi-ops.github.io/M13-Skeletal-Notes/shorttitlelowercase.html">skeletal notes</url> <mdash/>
            see, for example,
            <url href="https://mahmadi-ops.github.io/M13-Skeletal-Notes/worksheet-assignment-1.html">Assignment 1</url>. An
            assignment is posted once we have covered the topics it draws on,
            so check the skeletal notes regularly. Each assignment page also
            carries the AI tutor described in <xref ref="ai-tutor"/>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <em>Where and when to find the solutions:</em> the solutions to an
            assignment are added to that same worksheet page in the skeletal
            notes, and they go up
            <alert>only after the assignment's due date has passed</alert>. The
            review problem sets, such as
            <url href="https://mahmadi-ops.github.io/M13-Skeletal-Notes/worksheet-review-problems-1.html">Review Problems #1</url>,
            are different: they are not collected, so their solutions are there
            from the start.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Each assignment may consist of a few topics, which means time
            management will be very important. I highly recommend that you start
            working on the problems as soon as they are available.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <table xml:id="table-assignment-days">
      <title>Due day for each assignment</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor">
        <col width="50%"/>
        <col width="50%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
          <cell>Assignments</cell>
          <cell>Due day, at 11:59 PM</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>1, 2, 4, 5, 7, 8, 10</cell>
          <cell>Friday</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>3, 6, 9</cell>
          <cell>Wednesday (exam weeks 3, 6, and 9)</cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

  
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="gradescope">
    <title>Submitting Your Assignments in Gradescope</title>

    <figure xml:id="fig-gradescope">
      <caption>Write your solutions by hand, scan them into a single PDF, then
      assign pages to questions in Gradescope.</caption>
      <image source="gradescope-submission.png" width="88%">
        <shortdescription>Handwritten pages are scanned with a phone into a single PDF, then submitted</shortdescription>
        <description>
          <p>
            A three-step cartoon. On the left, a stack of handwritten pages
            covered in sketches of axes, surfaces, and integrals. An arrow
            points to the middle, where a phone scans one of the pages, a bright
            line sweeping down the screen. A second arrow points to the right,
            where the scanned pages have become a tidy stack of PDF pages, each
            one tagged with a colored marker showing that it has been assigned
            to a question. A student gives a thumbs up beside a large green
            check mark.
          </p>
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Assignments will <alert>not</alert> be submitted in Camino or
            Edfinity. You will submit each assignment in
            <alert>Gradescope</alert> as a single PDF file. You can access
            Gradescope through the link in our Camino course.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            For step-by-step instructions on how to scan and submit your work,
            please watch
            <url href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nksyA0s-Geo">this short video on submitting an assignment in Gradescope</url>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <alert>Important:</alert> when you upload your PDF, you
            <alert>must assign pages to each question</alert>. Assign to each
            question only the page or pages that contain your work for that
            question <mdash/> do not assign every page to every question, and do
            not leave any question unassigned. Work that is not assigned to the
            corresponding question can be missed by the grader. The
            <url href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nksyA0s-Geo">video above</url>
            shows you how to assign pages, step by step.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Make sure that your scan is legible and that the pages are in order
            before you submit. It is your responsibility to check that your
            submission was uploaded correctly before the deadline.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </subsection>
</section>
